        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#short-verdict" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. The Short Verdict</a>
          <a href="#who-makes-them" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Who Makes Them</a>
          <a href="#platforms" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. The Battery Platforms</a>
          <a href="#drills" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Drill Head to Head</a>
          <a href="#impact-drivers" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Impact Drivers and Kits</a>
          <a href="#batteries-warranty" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Batteries and Warranty</a>
          <a href="#cost-verdict" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Cost and Who Should Buy</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          If you maintain a house, buy Ryobi ONE+ HP and spend the difference on more tools. If you use a drill for a living, or you want one platform that will survive a decade of hard use and be on the shelf at every hardware store in the country, buy DeWalt 20V MAX XR. On the spec sheet the gap between the two is far smaller than the price gap suggests. In service life, warranty support and pro-grade options, it is real.
        </p>

        <div id="short-verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Verdict
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ryobi and DeWalt are the two cordless brands most first-time buyers end up choosing between, and they are aimed at different people. Ryobi builds for homeowners and hobbyists who use a drill a few dozen times a year. DeWalt builds primarily for contractors, framers and remodelers who run tools all day, and sells the same platform to homeowners who want something that will outlast them. Almost every honest answer to the question falls out of that difference.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the typical buyer, someone assembling furniture, hanging shelves, building a deck over a few weekends and keeping a house in repair, Ryobi ONE+ HP is the better purchase. Its brushless HP drill and impact driver are close enough to DeWalt's XR tools on paper that you will not feel the difference in intermittent work, and a two-tool HP kit costs roughly what a single DeWalt XR kit costs on sale. That is why the compact Ryobi HP drill is the budget pick in our <a href="/best-cordless-drills/">best cordless drills guide</a>, and why we would recommend it to a friend without hesitation.
          </p>

          <p>
            For anyone who earns money with tools, DeWalt is the better purchase, and the reason is not peak power. It is what happens on the fiftieth hole of the day, how the chuck and clutch hold up in year three, a repair path through service centers, a 90-day satisfaction guarantee and a free service year on top of the warranty, and access to professional tools that Ryobi simply does not make, such as FLEXVOLT 60V MAX saws. DeWalt's XR drill is also the brand's pick in the same guide for buyers who want a lighter, kit-priced alternative to Milwaukee.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a middle buyer, and they are the hard case: the serious DIYer who renovates a room every year, does their own framing and wants to buy one platform for life. For that person DeWalt is usually worth the premium, because the tools they will grow into, from framing nailers to FLEXVOLT miter saws, sit on DeWalt's battery and not on Ryobi's. If that is you, read the cost section before deciding, because the total spend gap over five years is larger than the drill price gap suggests.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">On Paper, Side by Side</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">850 in-lbs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Ryobi HP PBLDD02 drill, maker's rating</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2,200 in-lbs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Ryobi HP PBLID02 impact, maker's rating</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1,825 in-lbs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">DeWalt DCF887 and DCF850 impact</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">3 years</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Tool warranty, both brands</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="who-makes-them" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Who Makes Them, and Where You Can Buy Them
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ryobi power tools in North America are run by Techtronic Industries, the Hong Kong-listed group that also owns Milwaukee. TTI bought Ryobi Limited's North American power tool business in 2000 and operates the brand under license; the Japanese Ryobi company that originally owned it now makes die castings and printing equipment and has no connection to the green tools at Home Depot. As we covered in <a href="/milwaukee-vs-ryobi/">Milwaukee vs Ryobi</a>, the shared parent does not mean shared engineering. The two brands have separate design teams and very different cost targets.
          </p>

          <p>
            DeWalt is owned by Stanley Black and Decker, which also owns Craftsman, Black and Decker and Stanley. Like TTI, SBD runs its brands at different price points, and DeWalt is the professional tier. That positioning shows up in the details: metal ratcheting chucks on the current XR drills compared below, a service center network, and a product catalog that includes jobsite tools a homeowner will never buy, such as cordless framing nailers, rotary hammers and 60V MAX table saws.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where you can buy them is a bigger practical difference than most comparisons admit. New Ryobi tools are sold in US stores only through Home Depot, under a long-standing exclusive arrangement, and Ryobi's own factory outlet sells reconditioned and blemished tools online. DeWalt is everywhere: Home Depot, Lowe's, Ace, Amazon, independent tool dealers and most lumberyards. If a battery dies on a Saturday afternoon in a small town, a DeWalt replacement is usually easier to find.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Home Depot exclusive has upsides too. Returns and warranty swaps in the first 90 days are handled at the returns desk, and Home Depot runs aggressive Ryobi promotions several times a year, including free-tool-with-battery deals that make the platform even cheaper than its list prices suggest. If you live near a Home Depot and buy during those promotions, the exclusivity costs you almost nothing. If you do not, it is a real consideration.
          </p>

          <p>
            One myth worth retiring: neither brand is a rebadge of the other, and neither brand's batteries fit the other's tools. Ryobi shares a parent with Milwaukee, not with DeWalt, and DeWalt's sister brand Craftsman uses its own V20 battery. When you choose between these two, you are choosing two completely separate battery ecosystems with no official crossover.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="platforms" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Battery Platforms: ONE+ and 40V vs 20V MAX, XR, Atomic and FLEXVOLT
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both main platforms are 18-volt systems. Ryobi calls its pack 18V ONE+; DeWalt calls its 20V MAX, which is the maximum initial voltage of the same five-cell lithium-ion layout, with a nominal voltage of 18 volts. The 20 on the label is marketing, not extra power, so ignore it when comparing the two.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ryobi's handheld platform is ONE+, and by Ryobi's own count it now spans over 300 products on a battery interface that has stayed compatible for 30 years. Within ONE+ there are two tiers: standard tools, many still brushed, and HP tools with brushless motors and better electronics. Buy HP wherever it exists; our <a href="/ryobi-one-plus-hp-vs-standard/">Ryobi ONE+ HP vs standard</a> breakdown explains why the premium is small and the difference is not. Ryobi's outdoor equipment runs mostly on a separate 40V platform, with more than 75 tools of its own and batteries that do not fit ONE+ tools. Our guide to <a href="/ryobi-18v-vs-40v/">Ryobi 18V vs 40V</a> covers when the second platform is worth it.
          </p>

          <p>
            DeWalt's platform is more layered. 20V MAX is the base system, which DeWalt says now includes more than 300 products. XR is the premium brushless tier within it, Atomic is a line of compact 20V MAX tools, and all three share the same batteries. FLEXVOLT batteries switch between 20V and 60V MAX depending on the tool, so a FLEXVOLT pack runs both a 60V MAX circular saw and an ordinary 20V MAX drill. Some newer 20V tools carry a FLEXVOLT ADVANTAGE label, meaning they draw extra power when a FLEXVOLT pack is installed. There is also a separate 12V MAX Xtreme compact platform, which shares nothing with 20V MAX.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm"></th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Ryobi</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">DeWalt</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Main handheld platform</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">18V ONE+ (HP = brushless tier)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20V MAX (XR = brushless premium, Atomic = compact)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Tool count claimed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Over 300 ONE+ products</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Over 300 20V MAX products</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Higher-voltage option</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">40V outdoor platform, separate batteries</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">FLEXVOLT 60V MAX, batteries also run 20V MAX tools</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Compact platform</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None separate; compact HP tools on ONE+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">12V MAX Xtreme, separate batteries</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Parent company</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Techtronic Industries (also Milwaukee)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stanley Black and Decker (also Craftsman)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Where sold new (US)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Home Depot</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Home Depot, Lowe's, Ace, Amazon, dealers</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Both brands claim more than 300 products, so tool count alone no longer separates them. What separates them is the kind of tools. Ryobi's catalog leans toward the household: glue guns, inflators, misting fans, wet-dry vacs, hobby rotary tools and a deep outdoor range. DeWalt's leans toward the jobsite: framing and finish nailers, rotary hammers, grinders, cordless table and miter saws and dust extraction. Look at the five tools you expect to buy after the drill, and the right platform usually becomes obvious; our <a href="/best-ryobi-one-plus-tools/">best Ryobi ONE+ tools</a> list shows where Ryobi's breadth is genuinely useful.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drills" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Drill Head to Head: What the Spec Sheets Say and What They Hide
          </h2>

          <p>
            The drill is where most people start, and it is the hardest place to compare these brands fairly, because they publish different numbers. Ryobi rates its drills in inch-pounds of torque. DeWalt's US pages rate them in unit watts out, a power figure measured DeWalt's own way, and do not list torque at all. DeWalt's European pages give torque in newton-metres. None of these are measured under one shared standard, so treat any cross-brand comparison as approximate.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Drill (maker's figures)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Rated output</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Speeds</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Length</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Ryobi ONE+ HP PSBDD02</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Compact brushless drill/driver</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Up to 450 in-lbs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0-450 / 0-1,700 RPM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6.1 in</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Ryobi ONE+ HP PBLDD02</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Full-size brushless drill/driver</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Up to 850 in-lbs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0-700 / 0-2,150 RPM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not listed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">DeWalt 20V MAX XR DCD800</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Brushless drill/driver</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">40% more UWO than DCD791; 90 Nm on EU spec</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0-650 / 0-2,000 RPM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6.37 in</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">DeWalt 20V MAX XR DCD805</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Brushless hammer drill</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">40% more UWO than DCD796; 34,000 BPM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0-650 / 0-2,000 RPM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7 in</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">DeWalt DCD999</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hammer drill, FLEXVOLT ADVANTAGE</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Up to 42% more power on a 6Ah FLEXVOLT pack</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-speed metal transmission</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not listed</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Read sensibly, the full-size drills are close. Ryobi's second-generation HP drill, the PBLDD02, is rated at up to 850 inch-pounds with a 24-position clutch and a metal ratcheting chuck. DeWalt's DCD800, the current compact XR drill/driver, is rated at 90 newton-metres in Europe, which converts to roughly 800 inch-pounds. That is not a meaningful gap for anyone driving deck screws or boring holes with a spade bit, and the Ryobi actually spins slightly faster in high gear. On paper alone, the cheaper drill holds its own.
          </p>

          <p>
            The compact drills are a different story. Ryobi's compact HP drill/driver, the PSBDD02, is rated at 450 inch-pounds and is genuinely small at 6.1 inches. It is the current version of the compact HP drill that is the budget pick in our <a href="/best-cordless-drills/">cordless drill rankings</a>, and it is plenty for furniture, shelving and pilot holes. But DeWalt's DCD800 is barely longer at 6.37 inches while being rated far higher, so if you want a small drill that can also bore big holes, DeWalt's compact is in a different class from Ryobi's.
          </p>

          <p>
            What the spec sheets hide is the stuff that shows up in month eight. In the boring tests behind our drill guide, the Ryobi's single-sleeve chuck loosened slightly under sustained heavy vibration, and its plastic gear housing is visibly lighter-duty than the metal-reinforced construction on pro drills. DeWalt's XR drills use metal ratcheting chucks and are built to be dropped off ladders. Neither difference matters for a drill used twice a month. Both matter for a drill used eight hours a day.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you need to drill masonry, both brands make hammer drills, and DeWalt's DCD805 and DCD999 are serious tools; the FLEXVOLT ADVANTAGE DCD999 is effectively DeWalt's answer to the question of how much power an 18V-class drill can make. Our <a href="/hammer-drill-vs-drill-driver/">hammer drill vs drill/driver</a> guide explains when you need one. Whichever brand you choose, buy brushless; <a href="/brushless-vs-brushed-drill/">brushless vs brushed</a> covers why the old brushed Ryobi and 20V MAX drills are no longer worth the small saving.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="impact-drivers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Impact Drivers and the Combo Kit Question
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most people should buy a drill and an impact driver together, because the impact driver does the heavy fastening that wears out a drill's clutch and your wrist. If you are not sure why you need both, our <a href="/drill-vs-impact-driver/">drill vs impact driver</a> explainer settles it in a few minutes. The good news is that both brands sell the pair as a kit, and the kit is almost always cheaper than buying the two tools separately.
          </p>

          <p>
            Here the spec sheet actually favors Ryobi. Its HP brushless four-mode impact driver, the PBLID02, is rated at up to 2,200 inch-pounds with four drive modes including an assist mode. DeWalt's 20V MAX XR three-speed DCF887 and its compact Atomic DCF850 are both rated at 1,825 inch-pounds, and the Atomic is under four inches long. On raw torque the Ryobi wins by about 20 percent; on size, the Atomic wins clearly, which matters when you are driving screws between joists or inside a cabinet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not read too much into the torque number. Peak impact torque matters for breaking loose rusted bolts and driving big lags, and 1,825 inch-pounds already handles 4-inch structural screws and lag bolts. What separates impact drivers in daily use is speed control, how well the low setting avoids snapping small screws, and how long the anvil and hammer mechanism last under thousands of cycles. DeWalt's three-speed control is mature and well liked; Ryobi's four-mode control is good and gives you more torque for the money. For more models in both brands, see our <a href="/best-impact-driver/">best impact driver</a> guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            The kits line up roughly like this. Ryobi's HP compact two-tool kit pairs the compact HP drill and impact driver with two batteries, a charger and a bag. Its full-size HP kit, the PBLCK01K, pairs the full-size HP drill and impact with two 2Ah batteries. DeWalt's DCK2051D2 pairs the DCD800 drill with the Atomic DCF850 impact and two 2Ah packs, and the premium DCK2100P2 that is the combo pick in our drill guide pairs the DCD999 hammer drill with the DCF887 impact and two 5Ah batteries. Prices are in the cost section below.
          </p>

          <p>
            One honest note on the cheaper kits. Both brands still sell brushed drill and impact kits at the bottom of their ranges, such as DeWalt's long-running DCK240C2 with 1.3Ah packs and Ryobi's basic ONE+ kits. They are fine for occasional use, but the batteries are tiny and the tools are a generation behind. If the budget allows, the brushless HP or XR kit is the better buy from either brand.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="batteries-warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Batteries, Compatibility, Warranty and Service
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ryobi's battery story is its strongest card. Every ONE+ battery fits every ONE+ tool, back to the platform's launch in 1996, so a drill bought tomorrow runs on a pack from a garage sale. HP tools reach full output only with HP or High Performance packs, but older packs still work at reduced power. Our <a href="/are-ryobi-one-plus-batteries-interchangeable/">Ryobi battery compatibility guide</a> covers the edge cases, and <a href="/how-long-do-ryobi-batteries-last/">how long Ryobi batteries last</a> explains what actually kills them.
          </p>

          <p>
            DeWalt's compatibility is also excellent within 20V MAX. Every 20V MAX battery runs every 20V MAX, XR and Atomic tool, and FLEXVOLT packs run them too, which means one large FLEXVOLT pack can power a table saw in the morning and a drill in the afternoon. DeWalt's newer POWERSTACK packs, which use stacked pouch cells instead of cylindrical ones, also fit the same tools. The limits are at the edges: 12V MAX Xtreme is separate, and 60V MAX tools need FLEXVOLT packs, not ordinary 20V ones.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Coverage</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Ryobi</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">DeWalt</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Handheld tools</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-year limited (ONE+)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-year limited</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Batteries and chargers</td>
