                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            On paper the warranties look similar: three years on tools and three years on batteries from both brands. The differences are in the extras and in how claims work. DeWalt adds one year of free service, covering maintenance and worn parts that a warranty would not, and a 90-day satisfaction guarantee. Ryobi's extra is the 5-year warranty on 40V outdoor tools, which matters if you are buying a mower or a chainsaw on that platform.
          </p>

          <p>
            Service is where trade users should pay attention. DeWalt tools are designed to be repaired, with factory and authorized service centers, published parts and replaceable switches and chucks. Ryobi tools are, in practice, replaced rather than repaired: a warranty claim usually ends with a new tool, and out of warranty the repair cost tends to exceed the price of a replacement. For a homeowner that is a perfectly reasonable arrangement. For a contractor whose drill cannot be out of service for three weeks, the repair path is worth paying for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost-verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What It Actually Costs, and Who Should Buy Which
          </h2>

          <p>
            Prices on both brands move constantly with promotions, so treat these as typical ranges between sale and list prices rather than fixed figures. The pattern is consistent, though: for a comparable brushless drill and impact kit, Ryobi costs roughly half to two-thirds of what DeWalt does, and the gap widens as you add batteries.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
