          <p>
            The kit price is only the start. The real cost of a platform is the next five tools and the extra batteries, and that is where Ryobi's advantage compounds. Ryobi bare tools are cheaper, its batteries are cheaper, and Home Depot regularly bundles a free bare tool with a battery purchase. A homeowner building out a drill, impact, circular saw, reciprocating saw, shop vac and string trimmer will typically spend hundreds of dollars less on ONE+ than on 20V MAX. On the other side, DeWalt tools hold resale value and Ryobi tools have almost none, which narrows the gap for anyone who sells their old gear.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Count hours per week, not dollars per tool.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Under a few hours a week of use, the extra durability you pay DeWalt for is measured in duty cycles you will never reach, so Ryobi HP is the better deal. At daily professional use, DeWalt's tougher build, repair path and pro tool range pay back the premium within a year or two.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Buy Ryobi ONE+ HP if you are a homeowner, renter or hobbyist, your projects are intermittent, you want household and outdoor tools on the same battery, and you live near a Home Depot. It is the platform we recommend most often in our <a href="/best-cordless-drill-for-home-use/">best cordless drill for home use</a> guide and in our full <a href="/ryobi-tools-review/">Ryobi tools review</a>, and for that buyer it is not a compromise.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy DeWalt 20V MAX XR if you work in the trades, you are a serious renovator who will grow into framing nailers and FLEXVOLT saws, you want the most compact pro-grade drill and impact, or you need batteries available at any hardware store. If you are still deciding between the professional brands, our <a href="/milwaukee-vs-dewalt/">Milwaukee vs DeWalt</a> and <a href="/dewalt-vs-makita/">DeWalt vs Makita</a> comparisons take it from there, and our ranking of the <a href="/best-cordless-tool-brand/">best cordless tool brands</a> puts all of them in context.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing torque numbers across brands</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ryobi publishes inch-pounds, DeWalt's US site publishes unit watts out and its European site uses newton-metres. None are tested to one shared standard, so a 50 in-lb difference between brands tells you almost nothing. Compare durability features, chuck and clutch quality, and warranty terms instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the brushed bottom-tier kit to save fifty dollars</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Both brands still sell brushed drill and impact kits with tiny batteries. The brushless HP or XR kit costs a little more, runs cooler, lasts longer and comes with better packs. Unless the drill will genuinely live in a drawer, the step up pays for itself.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing a platform by the drill alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The drill is the first of many tools. If the next five purchases are a mower, a trimmer, a glue gun and a shop vac, Ryobi wins. If they are a framing nailer, a rotary hammer and a miter saw, DeWalt wins. Decide based on that list.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Splitting your kit across both brands</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Running a Ryobi drill and a DeWalt saw means two chargers, two sets of batteries and two places to buy spares. Third-party battery adapters exist but bypass the electronics that protect pack and tool. Pick one platform for handheld tools and stay on it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Ryobi and DeWalt share a parent</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ryobi shares a parent company with Milwaukee, not DeWalt, and DeWalt's sister brand is Craftsman. No Ryobi battery fits a DeWalt tool or the other way round, and shared ownership elsewhere tells you nothing about how either brand's tools perform.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Ryobi as good as DeWalt?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For homeowner use, Ryobi ONE+ HP tools are close enough that you will not notice the difference, and Ryobi's HP impact driver is rated at higher torque than DeWalt's XR models. For daily professional use, DeWalt is better: tougher chucks and housings, a repair network, a free service year and a much deeper range of jobsite tools.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which is better, a Ryobi or DeWalt drill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For the money, Ryobi. Its full-size HP drill, the PBLDD02, is rated at up to 850 in-lbs, close to DeWalt's DCD800 at about 800 in-lbs converted from its 90 Nm European rating. DeWalt's drill is more compact for its power and better built for heavy daily use, so it is the better drill if cost is not the deciding factor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do Ryobi batteries fit DeWalt tools?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Ryobi ONE+ and DeWalt 20V MAX use different battery interfaces and electronics, even though both are 18-volt nominal systems. Third-party adapters are sold, but they bypass the communication between pack and tool that protects both, and they can complicate a warranty claim. Pick one platform for your handheld tools.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who owns Ryobi and DeWalt?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Ryobi power tools in North America are run by Techtronic Industries under license from the Japanese Ryobi company; TTI also owns Milwaukee. DeWalt is owned by Stanley Black and Decker, which also owns Craftsman, Black and Decker and Stanley. The two brands are not related and share no parts or batteries.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is DeWalt 20V MAX more powerful than Ryobi 18V?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. DeWalt's 20V MAX figure is the maximum initial voltage of an 18-volt nominal pack, the same nominal voltage as Ryobi ONE+. Power differences come from motors and electronics, not the voltage label. DeWalt's real high-voltage advantage is FLEXVOLT 60V MAX, which powers saws and other heavy tools Ryobi's ONE+ platform does not match.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I buy Ryobi anywhere besides Home Depot?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                New Ryobi tools are sold in US stores only at Home Depot, which also sells them online. Ryobi's factory outlet sells reconditioned and blemished tools online with a warranty, and some third-party sellers list Ryobi on marketplaces. DeWalt is sold at Home Depot, Lowe's, Ace, Amazon and most independent tool dealers.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do the Ryobi and DeWalt warranties compare?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both cover tools and batteries for three years. DeWalt adds a year of free service and a 90-day money-back guarantee, and its tools are designed to be repaired at service centers. Ryobi gives 5 years on its 40V outdoor tools, and its warranty claims usually end in a replacement rather than a repair.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

